  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 153 PM MDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nSatellite imagery shows upper low centered over Washington this\nafternoon, with deep S-SW flow in place over East Idaho. Breezy\nwinds still developing across the region today, strongest at\nhigher elevations across the central mountains, and expected to\ncontinue tonight as the associated frontal boundary passes\nthrough the region. Moisture may shifts close enough to provide\nlight showers across portions of the Frank Church, but otherwise\ndry conditions are expected to continue. Much cooler\ntemperatures arrive behind the front, with daytime highs in the\n60s for most lower elevation areas, though a few locations along\nthe Utah border may still reach into the 70s. The next system\nmoves inland Sunday, bringing mainly isolated showers and\nthunderstorms to higher elevation regions Sunday and Monday.\nEnsemble means maintain shallow high pressure across Idaho\nthrough most of the week, allowing temperatures to remain near\nto slightly below normal through the week, despite a brief\nrebound Sunday. There is potential for break down of the high by\nthe weekend, as the ensemble means bring a trough through the\nregion Friday into Saturday. The operational GFS is\nsignificantly more pronounced with this feature than the\noperational ECMWF.",
